WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.423
IEC 62443 organizes technical security objectives into seven foundational requirements.

00:00:04.423 --> 00:00:09.179
Together they provide a balanced view of identity, authorization, integrity, confidentiality,

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:11.340
communication, detection, response, and availability.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.528
The required strength of each objective depends on the zone, conduit, risk,

00:00:16.528 --> 00:00:18.257
and selected security level.

00:00:18.257 --> 00:00:24.742
Identification and authentication control establishes who or what is requesting access and verifies that identity.

00:00:24.742 --> 00:00:30.794
Use control determines what an authenticated user, device, or process is allowed to do.

00:00:30.794 --> 00:00:33.388
Industrial systems may have technical limitations,

00:00:33.388 --> 00:00:39.441
so architecture and managed gateways may need to enforce controls that individual devices cannot.

00:00:39.441 --> 00:00:42.034
System integrity protects systems, software, configurations,

00:00:42.034 --> 00:00:45.061
and communications from unauthorized or accidental change.

00:00:45.061 --> 00:00:50.248
Data confidentiality protects information from unauthorized disclosure where that disclosure creates risk.

00:00:50.248 --> 00:00:51.978
Confidentiality matters in OT,

00:00:51.978 --> 00:00:57.165
but it must be implemented without undermining required visibility, timing, or recovery.

00:00:57.165 --> 00:01:01.489
Restricted data flow limits communication between zones and through conduits.

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:07.109
It supports segmentation and containment by allowing necessary flows while denying unnecessary paths.

00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:11.864
Timely response to events addresses the ability to detect, record, communicate,

00:01:11.864 --> 00:01:14.026
and respond to security-relevant activity.

00:01:14.026 --> 00:01:17.484
Useful monitoring requires ownership, context, and practiced procedures.

00:01:17.484 --> 00:01:22.672
Resource availability protects the capacity and functions needed for safe, reliable operation.

00:01:22.672 --> 00:01:26.130
This includes resilience against resource exhaustion, component failure,

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.860
and disruptive security controls.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.751
Redundancy, backup, recovery, and graceful degradation may all contribute.

00:01:31.751 --> 00:01:35.641
The foundational requirements should be engineered as a system.

00:01:35.641 --> 00:01:40.829
Strong authentication without availability planning can lock operators out during an emergency.

00:01:40.829 --> 00:01:44.288
Excellent logging without response ownership produces little value.

00:01:44.288 --> 00:01:50.340
The objective is a set of mutually supporting controls aligned with the industrial process.
